            <div xml:id="d514">

                <pb facs="0734" n="632" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg632"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/875: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Marriner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-08-19T19:00:00+01:00">August 19, 1935—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 19—4 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>711. French press reactions to the break-up of the tripartite negotiations show
                    little sign of being influenced by internal political considerations. From left
                    to right the newspapers express sorrow at the inability of the three powers to
                    arrive at conclusion and show a tendency to place the principal cause for
                    failure upon the divergence between Anglo-Italian viewpoints. This allows them
                    to show France as the disappointed peacemaker. If anything the general tendency
                    is to infer that Italy should be leniently and considerately treated without
                    compromising France’s devotion and duty to the League.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marriner</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
